  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study analyzes public comments and interviews with emotional support animal (ESA) owners to critique the framing of these animals' roles as labor. By examining relationships with non-dog ESAs, the research contrasts labor-centric legal definitions with the lived, non-hierarchical dynamics of human-animal companionship.",
      "why": "It challenges the prevailing legal and sociological practice of using 'affective labor' to legitimize ESAs, offering instead a philosophical framework based on Spinozan ethics and Deleuzian 'becoming-animal' to understand human-animal relationships without reducing them to utility or work."
